  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nThere will remain a low chance of showers across far southeast \nOklahoma Sunday morning within the deep moist flow but backed off \nfrom model blend values based on bias of CAMs producing more \nwidespread and heavier activity in this pattern. Atmosphere looks to \nremain capped through much of the day across the remainder of the \nforecast area with a strong EML still present on forecast soundings. \nWe will have to watch stronger activity that develops across \nnorthwest and north-central Oklahoma along the dryline during the \nevening as it may approach the area. Otherwise, expect broad lift\nto begin to encroach on the area late evening and overnight as \nshortwave activity moves through the southwest flow aloft. Steep \nlapse rates and drier mid-level air will result in a hail and wind\nthreat from the stronger activity. Winds will be breezy from \nearly morning on with near advisory gusts across northeast OK. \nLater shifts may need to add wind headlines for Sunday and \npossibly Monday as the strongest low level wind fields remain \nacross this area. \n\nAs the upper level trough takes on more of a negative tilt and moves \ninto the central Rockies on Monday, a sharpening dryline and cold\nfront will become the focus for activity to our west and \nnorthwest. Once again with cloud cover and a capping inversion \nexpect storm coverage to be somewhat limited early Monday. Though \nshort term guidance is consistent with a shortwave and \naccompanying jet streak lifting out of northeast Texas in the \nmorning which could help increase coverage and intensity from \nsoutheast Oklahoma into west- central Arkansas. Maintained chance \nPoPs Monday afternoon into the evening across the entire area with\nthe best forcing and boundary focus still to our west and \nnorthwest. Moderate to strong instability within the open warm \nsector and strong deep layer shear and lapse rates will keep the \npotential for higher end severe weather across the area and this \nis more likely be realized if the cap remains in place and daytime\nshowers and storms remain limited. \n\nMore widespread showers and storms are expected, however, to spread \nacross northeast Oklahoma Monday night into Tuesday as the cold \nfront slide south across the area. Guidance remains somewhat split \nstill on how fast the front will clear the area with the slowest \nensemble members keeping the front near I-40 through Wednesday \nmorning. If the front were to hold up, there will be an increased \nchance of severe weather and locally heavy rainfall across \nsoutheast Oklahoma into northwest Arkansas on Tuesday. With the \nsynoptic front south of the area by Wednesday and cooler\nconditions the severe weather threat will diminish though a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall lingers south of I-40 through\nthe afternoon.\n\nSplit flow aloft develops with the exit of the main trough late \nTuesday. This will maintain southwest flow aloft through the \nremainder of the week with more shortwave activity bringing \noccasional shower and storm chances especially as low level \nmoisture returns late in the week.",
